{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-332270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4x+2.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x+\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x+(\\alpha +2\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9567f634c202807d6b856ab0430f660c8ec691300d151bd7fb98d165273d7f8c"} {"question" "q-332270" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332270" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Ae^{-2x}+2x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=-10.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-2\\alpha =-10\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9567f634c202807d6b856ab0430f660c8ec691300d151bd7fb98d165273d7f8c"} {"question" "q-332292" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332292" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-6x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-6x-7\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(\\alpha +6\\beta )=-6x-7.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +6\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9567f634c202807d6b856ab0430f660c8ec691300d151bd7fb98d165273d7f8c"} {"question" "q-332307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-x-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=5x-4.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=5x-4\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n5\\alpha x+(\\alpha +5\\beta )=5x-4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +5\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+x-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332539" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9567f634c202807d6b856ab0430f660c8ec691300d151bd7fb98d165273d7f8c"} {"question" "q-332539" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-332539" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Ae^{-5x}+x-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-333042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-333042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -4\\beta )x+(\\beta -4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =-2\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -4\\beta =5\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -4\\gamma =-3\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},$ $\\beta =-1,$ and $\\gamma =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4(0)}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}-(0)+\\frac{1}{2} & =-1 \\\\ A+\\frac{1}{2} & =-1 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-333042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9567f634c202807d6b856ab0430f660c8ec691300d151bd7fb98d165273d7f8c"} {"question" "q-333042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-333042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+1"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "y={e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"} {"question" "q-333042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064870/state.json" "file_sha256" "38d142b6d133c9528152a0e9026657f2bae1b38620474085316b8d882223b8a6"}] "usage" {"q-332270" []} "basis" 2122 "retractions" [[17592186229579 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y = 4x + 2$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186229579 :question/worked-solution "Try $y_p=ax+b$. Substitution into $y^{\\prime}+2y=4x+2$ gives $2ax+(a+2b)=4x+2$, so $a=2$ and $b=0$. Thus $y_p=2x$. Adding the supplied complementary solution gives $y(x)=Ae^{-2x}+2x$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186238169] "immutable_fields" [[17592186228012 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228012 :answer-field/id] [17592186228012 :answer-field/key] [17592186228012 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
